All header modifications within a transaction share the same commit frame. Evidence:

1. Single commit point:
   - prepare_collected_frames() (wal.rs:2002-2008):
     ```rust
     let frame_db_size = if idx + 1 == pages.len() {
         db_size_on_commit.unwrap_or(0)
     } else {
         0
     };
     ```
   - Only the LAST frame of a transaction has db_size > 0 (commit marker)
   - All frames in a commit batch share the same epoch

2. Dirty pages batched together:
   - commit_dirty_pages_inner() collects ALL dirty pages at once (pager.rs:3531)
   - They are converted to frames in one PrepareFrames phase
   - Written to WAL together in WaitWrites phase

3. Visibility atomicity:
   - max_frame is updated AFTER fsync (wal.rs:2045-2046)
   - `commit_prepared_frames()` advances max_frame only after all writes are durable
   - Readers see either old max_frame OR new max_frame, never partial

4. Recovery respects commit boundary:
   - process_frames() (sqlite3_ondisk.rs:1706-1709):
     ```rust
     if db_size > 0 {
         st.last_valid_frame = st.frame_idx;
         st.last_valid_checksum = calc;
         self.flush_pending_frames(&mut st);
     }
     ```
   - Only frames up to last commit are made visible

Therefore, all header changes (in page 1) and data changes in a transaction become visible atomically.
